The 30 SQL performance tips
1. Select only the columns you actually need. Avoid SELECT * when your application needs only a few columns. For example, prefer SELECT CustomerID, Name FROM Customers over SELECT * FROM Customers. This reduces data read from pages, memory usage, network transfer, and sometimes allows SQL Server to satisfy the query entirely from a narrower nonclustered index. This matters much more when the table has wide columns such as varchar(max), JSON, XML, or many columns.
2. Avoid unnecessary DISTINCT and ORDER BY. DISTINCT may require a Sort or Hash Aggregate to remove duplicates, while ORDER BY often introduces a Sort. Both can consume memory and spill to tempdb on large datasets. Use them only when the result actually requires uniqueness or ordering. In the execution plan, check for Sort, Hash Match (Aggregate), memory grants, and spill warnings.
3. Limit rows when exploring data. Instead of running SELECT * FROM Orders against a 100-million-row table just to inspect it, use SELECT TOP (10) * FROM Orders. The optimizer may introduce a Top operator and can stop once enough qualifying rows are obtained. This is mostly a development/exploration habit rather than production query optimization.
4. Index columns that are frequently used for filtering. If queries constantly do WHERE OrderStatus = 'Delivered', an index such as CREATE INDEX IX_Orders_OrderStatus ON Orders(OrderStatus); may allow an Index Seek instead of scanning the entire table. But don't automatically index every WHERE column. Selectivity matters: an index on a column containing only Yes/No might not help if half the table qualifies.
5. Avoid applying functions to indexed columns in predicates when possible. For example, WHERE YEAR(OrderDate) = 2025 may prevent an efficient seek on an OrderDate index. Prefer a searchable or SARGable predicate such as WHERE OrderDate >= '20250101' AND OrderDate < '20260101'. Similarly, instead of SUBSTRING(FirstName,1,1) = 'A', use FirstName LIKE 'A%'. This is one of the most valuable tips in the video.
6. Avoid leading wildcards when you need index seeking. WHERE LastName LIKE '%gold%' generally cannot seek efficiently through a normal B-tree index because SQL Server doesn't know where the matching string begins. WHERE LastName LIKE 'gold%' can often use an index much more efficiently. That doesn't mean %gold% is wrong; if the business requirement is "contains gold anywhere", then you need that semantics and may need a different search technology or indexing strategy.
7. Prefer IN over long chains of equality ORs for readability. Instead of CustomerID = 1 OR CustomerID = 2 OR CustomerID = 3, write CustomerID IN (1,2,3). One correction to the video's wording: this is not guaranteed to be faster. SQL Server frequently transforms both forms into essentially the same execution plan. The major guaranteed benefit is readability; verify performance in the plan.
8. Use INNER JOIN when you only need matching rows. If unmatched rows aren't required, don't use LEFT JOIN just out of habit. INNER JOIN communicates the correct semantics and gives the optimizer more freedom. But don't think "INNER JOIN is always faster than LEFT JOIN." The correct join type must come from your business requirement first. Changing LEFT JOIN to INNER JOIN can change the result.
9. Use explicit ANSI joins. Prefer FROM Customers c INNER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID instead of the old syntax FROM Customers c, Orders o WHERE c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID. SQL Server can normally produce the same plan, so this is mainly about correctness, maintainability, and making join conditions easier to identify.
10. Index important join columns. Suppose Customers.CustomerID is a primary key but Orders.CustomerID is an unindexed foreign key. An index on Orders(CustomerID) may substantially improve queries joining orders to customers. This can help SQL Server choose efficient Nested Loops, Merge Join, or other access paths. Foreign keys themselves do not automatically create indexes in SQL Server.
11. For very large or complex queries, reduce unnecessary data before joining — but test it. The video shows WHERE, putting a condition inside the join, or using a CTE/subquery first. SQL Server frequently pushes predicates down automatically, meaning all versions can generate the same plan. A CTE does not inherently "filter first." Use a CTE/subquery when it improves logic/readability or when restructuring genuinely changes the plan; verify in the execution plan.
12. Aggregate before joining when that significantly reduces rows. Suppose Orders has 100 million rows but you eventually need only one aggregate row per customer. Instead of joining all 100 million Orders rows to Customers and then grouping, it can sometimes be beneficial to aggregate Orders first: SELECT CustomerID, COUNT(*) FROM Orders GROUP BY CustomerID, then join that result to Customers. If 100 million rows become 500,000 customer rows before the join, you've dramatically reduced the join workload. Again, SQL Server can sometimes perform similar transformations itself, so test.
13. Consider splitting an OR in a join into separate joins combined with UNION/UNION ALL. A condition such as ON o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID OR o.SalesPersonID = c.CustomerID can make efficient index usage difficult. Sometimes two independently optimized queries allow indexes on each condition. However, be careful with duplicate semantics. UNION removes duplicates; UNION ALL doesn't. This rewrite is worthwhile only when the actual plan proves it helps.
14. Investigate unexpected Nested Loops on very large inputs, but don't casually force join hints. Nested Loops are excellent when the outer input is small and the inner side has an efficient seek. They can become disastrous if SQL Server expects 10 outer rows but actually receives 10 million. The first thing to investigate is usually why the estimate is wrong: statistics, predicates, parameter sniffing, data skew, etc. OPTION (HASH JOIN) can force a hash join, but hints should normally be a last resort because you're overriding the optimizer.
15. Use UNION ALL instead of UNION when duplicate elimination isn't required. UNION conceptually performs UNION ALL plus duplicate removal. Duplicate removal may require sorting or hashing. So if duplicates are acceptable or impossible by design, UNION ALL avoids unnecessary work. This is a very solid optimization rule.
16. For huge data, test UNION ALL followed by DISTINCT against UNION, but don't assume it's faster. The video recommends something like SELECT DISTINCT ... FROM (query1 UNION ALL query2) x. SQL Server may generate essentially the same plan as normal UNION, and sometimes the rewritten version can be worse. Treat this as an experiment, not a universal best practice.
17. Consider columnstore indexes for analytical aggregation over very large tables. For fact tables containing millions or hundreds of millions of rows, columnstore can be extremely effective for queries such as SUM, COUNT, AVG, and large scans. Instead of reading entire rows, SQL Server can read relevant columns, use strong compression, and often execute operators in batch mode. This is particularly suited to data warehouse/analytics workloads, not automatically OLTP tables.
18. Pre-aggregate expensive reporting data when real-time results aren't required. If a report takes five minutes because it repeatedly aggregates hundreds of millions of rows, you can materialize summarized results into a reporting table. For example, create daily sales totals once and let dashboards query those totals. The trade-off is freshness: you now need an ETL/job/process to keep the summary table synchronized.
19. When the purpose is existence testing, consider EXISTS. Suppose you need Orders only for customers from the USA and don't need any Customer columns. EXISTS expresses the idea clearly: "Return this order if a matching USA customer exists." SQL Server can implement this using a semi-join and stop looking once a match is found. The video's claim that IN is bad is too strong: modern SQL Server often generates equivalent plans for IN and EXISTS. NULL semantics also differ in certain NOT IN scenarios, so understand correctness first.
20. Eliminate redundant logic and repeated table access. If a query scans Employees four times and calculates the same AVG(Salary) twice, try restructuring it. A window expression such as AVG(Salary) OVER() can calculate the overall average while retaining individual employee rows. CTEs, window functions, conditional aggregation, and CASE often let you express the same result with fewer repeated operations. In the plan, look for repeated scans of the same large object.
21. Use the correct data type for the data. Don't store birthdays as varchar, scores as strings, or numeric values as text. Use date for dates, int/appropriate numeric types for numbers, etc. This improves data integrity, storage efficiency, comparisons, indexing, and avoids implicit conversions. One correction: varchar itself is not a bad datatype. It is correct for variable-length non-Unicode text. The old SQL Server text datatype is deprecated; use varchar(max) when truly large text is needed.
22. Avoid unnecessarily large column lengths. Don't automatically define everything as varchar(max) or varchar(255). If a column realistically needs around 50 characters, choose a suitable bounded length such as varchar(50). Wider definitions can increase memory estimates, row sizes, sort/hash memory needs, and index key limitations. However, don't choose a dangerously small length merely for performance; model the domain correctly.
23. Use NOT NULL when the business rule says a value must exist. NOT NULL primarily improves data integrity, and it can occasionally help the optimizer because SQL Server knows the column cannot contain nulls. It also simplifies queries because you don't need defensive IS NOT NULL checks where null values are impossible. Don't force NOT NULL merely as a performance trick if null is a meaningful business state.
24. Give tables appropriate keys, but don't blindly require every primary key to be clustered. A primary key gives uniqueness and is fundamental to relational design. SQL Server often creates the primary key as clustered by default when no clustered index exists, but the video's statement that every table should have a clustered primary key is too broad. Sometimes another column—such as an increasing date/key or a different access pattern—makes a better clustered index, while the PK remains nonclustered.
25. Consider nonclustered indexes on frequently used foreign keys. If Orders.CustomerID is regularly used for joins, filtering, deletes from parent tables, or lookups, indexing it is often very beneficial. Example: CREATE INDEX IX_Orders_CustomerID ON Orders(CustomerID);. Again, don't create one automatically for every foreign key. Check whether the workload actually uses it.
26. Avoid over-indexing. Every additional index speeds some reads but adds cost to INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE, consumes disk/storage and memory, and requires maintenance. If an Orders row is inserted and the table has eight indexes, SQL Server potentially has eight additional structures to update. The goal isn't "maximum indexes"; it's a small set of useful indexes supporting important workloads.
27. Monitor index usage and remove genuinely unused/redundant indexes carefully. SQL Server DMVs can show seeks, scans, lookups, and updates. An index receiving millions of updates and almost no reads may be questionable. But don't drop an index merely because it appears unused during a short observation window: it could support monthly reporting, year-end processes, or rarely executed critical queries. Also remember DMV counters reset after restarts and other events.
28. Keep optimizer statistics sufficiently current. SQL Server statistics describe data distribution and help estimate how many rows predicates will return. If SQL Server estimates 10 rows when reality is 10 million, it may select an inappropriate join, memory grant, or access method. Auto statistics handles a lot automatically, so you shouldn't blindly update every statistic every week on every database. But stale statistics are one of the first things to investigate when estimated and actual row counts differ substantially.
29. Maintain indexes based on actual fragmentation/workload — don't blindly rebuild everything weekly. The video's blanket "rebuild/reorganize every week" advice is outdated as a universal rule. Rebuilding large indexes consumes CPU, I/O, log space, and may be completely unnecessary. Fragmentation matters mainly in certain workloads, especially larger indexes and range scans. Measure first. REORGANIZE and REBUILD also have different costs and effects, and rebuilding generally refreshes associated statistics.
30. Partition genuinely very large tables when partitioning solves a real problem. Partitioning divides a logical table into partitions, often by date, such as monthly/yearly Orders. It can improve manageability, archival/loading strategies, and queries that support partition elimination, for example WHERE OrderDate >= .... But partitioning is not a magic "make queries faster" switch. A poorly designed partitioned table can perform no better—or worse—than a normal table. For huge analytical fact tables, combining partitioning with columnstore can be powerful.
